    <briefdescription>
<para>Hash table specialisation for <computeroutput>(<ref refid="exptable_8h_acbad03742bc161b060402329a67aa889_1acbad03742bc161b060402329a67aa889" kindref="member">exponent</ref> vector, unsigned long)</computeroutput> pairs. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Specialises David R. Hanson&apos;s generic C <computeroutput>Table_T</computeroutput> (declared in <computeroutput><ref refid="table_8h" kindref="compound">table.h</ref></computeroutput>) for the case the engine cares about most: keys are fixed-length integer exponent vectors and values are <computeroutput>unsigned long</computeroutput>. The <computeroutput>exponent_table_*</computeroutput> API wraps the generic operations <mdash/> <computeroutput>_new(hint, nvars)</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>_put</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>_get</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>_length</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>_free</computeroutput> <mdash/> with the right key-comparison and hashing for monomial exponents, so callers don&apos;t have to wire up <computeroutput>hash</computeroutput> / <computeroutput>cmp</computeroutput> callbacks themselves. The convention is to avoid storing zero values: <computeroutput>_put</computeroutput> returns the previously stored value (or <computeroutput>0</computeroutput> if absent), so a zero return uniformly means &quot;key was not present.&quot;</para>
<para>Used wherever the engine accumulates a sparse exponent-keyed histogram <mdash/> Hilbert-function buckets, monomial-coefficient tallies in res-monomials, F4 monomial-index pools. The base <computeroutput><ref refid="table_8h" kindref="compound">table.h</ref></computeroutput> predates the engine&apos;s C++ refactor and is one of the remaining C-only files in the engine.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="table_8h" kindref="compound">table.h</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
